A||||3||Jim's87GT||Transmission gasket and filter change||||1||
Z||000000||Jim's87GT||06-19-2000||07:55 AM||||The automatic transmission pan gasket on my car "weaps" a little.  I've decided to replace it and the filter, got the kit for about $8.70 but I have a couple of questions.  Do I put on any gasket sealer when I reinstall it?  What kind of torque should I use when tightining the bolts (using the wrist-ometer).  I've been lucky so far in the leak department, it seems that the only two places there are problems are the tranny gasket and the motor oil drain plug.  I got a new plug and nylon washer and I hope to be a good non-spotting citizen soon.  News at eleven.  Thanks ||||reg||1
Z||000001||deceler8||06-19-2000||10:09 AM||||No need to use gasket sealer. The gasket should be the type with snug bolt holes that use the bolts to hold the gasket in place during the install.<P>I used the "screwdriver" handle with the appropriate socket size to tighten the bolts by hand. Go over them several times and get them snug by hand (don't need a torque wrench) and that should do it.||||reg||1
Z||000002||dogtired||06-21-2000||03:05 AM||||I used a little copper-coat sealer on one side of the gasket, (pan side) probably not really necessary, but just makes me feel better.<BR>And like deceler8 said, wouldn't use a torque wrench just a calibrated wrist works well.<BR>The first time I replaced the gasket and filter I found a small round magnet that had a hole in the center in the bottom of the pan. It isn't glued down, just holds there by its own powers. Have since learned it is suppose to be there. Guess it is suppose to grab the chunks that fall off your tranny innards?? Anyway clean it all up good and put it back. I did my oil pan at the same time, what a fun weekend. Good Luck.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||Jim's87GT||06-21-2000||04:34 PM||||Well I finished my gasket and filter replacement task.  Not too bad.  All of the parts needed a good bath, I too found the magnet, cleaned it up and left it there. The inside of the tranny was nice and clean but the utside was caked with oily dirt from the leak generated by the dried up gasket.  All in all, worthwhile.  I'll say one thing, that job is a lot easier than trying to replace the serpentine belt.||||reg||1
